    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Generating short codes",
      "question": "How do you generate a short code that never collides?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. How do you generate a short code, and make sure two long URLs never get the same one? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "How do you generate a short code that <em>never collides</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Two approaches. Hash the long URL, and keep the first seven characters: simple, but different URLs can collide, so every write must check and retry. Or take a unique number from a counter, and encode it in base sixty-two. Unique by construction, with no collision checks.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Hash and check, or <em>count and encode</em>",
            "lang": "python",
            "left": {
              "at": "Hash",
              "label": "Hash + truncate",
              "title": "Collisions possible",
              "tone": "bad",
              "code": "code = md5(url).hexdigest()[:7]\nif db.exists(code):   # collision!\n    retry_with_salt()"
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "counter",
              "label": "Counter + Base62",
              "title": "Unique by construction",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "id = counter.next()   # 1000\ncode = base62(id)     # \"g8\""
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "construction",
              "text": "A unique ID encodes to a unique code: no checks.",
              "code": "id → [0-9a-zA-Z]"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Base sixty-two uses the digits, then lower case, then upper case letters. Repeatedly divide by sixty-two, and each remainder picks a character. For example, one thousand is sixteen sixty-twos plus eight, which encodes as g, eight. Seven characters cover three and a half trillion codes. At scale, the counter is a distributed ID service, or each server reserves a block of IDs, so writes never contend.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Base62: <em>remainders</em> pick characters",
            "file": "base62.py",
            "tag": "encode",
            "lang": "python",
            "size": 22,
            "width": 1120,
            "code": "ALPHABET = \"0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\"\n\ndef base62(n):\n    digits = []\n    while n:\n        n, r = divmod(n, 62)\n        digits.append(ALPHABET[r])\n    return \"\".join(reversed(digits)) or \"0\"\n\nbase62(1000)    # 1000 = 16·62 + 8 → \"g8\"\n62 ** 7         # 3.5 trillion 7-char codes\nblock = counter.reserve(1000)   # per-server ID range",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "digits",
                "lines": "1",
                "note": "0-9, a-z, A-Z: 62 URL-safe symbols"
              },
              {
                "at": "divide",
                "lines": "5-7",
                "note": "Each remainder picks one character"
              },
              {
                "at": "example",
                "lines": "10",
                "note": "16 → 'g', 8 → '8'"
              },
              {
                "at": "trillion",
                "lines": "11",
                "note": "62⁷ ≈ 3.5 × 10¹²"
              },
              {
                "at": "reserves",
                "lines": "12",
                "note": "Lease ID blocks: no shared hot counter"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Scaling the read path",
      "question": "Reads outnumber writes 100 to 1. How do you scale the service?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. Redirects outnumber new links a hundred to one. How do you scale the service? Pause and sketch it out.",
          "pauseAfter": 4,
          "props": {
            "question": "Reads outnumber writes <em>100 : 1</em>. How do you scale?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Start with a load balancer in front of a fleet of stateless web servers. Add a cache: popular codes stay hot, and least recently used eviction drops the rest, so most redirects never touch the database. Writes go to a primary, and read replicas absorb the cache misses. When the table outgrows one machine, shard it by short code. And record clicks asynchronously, through a queue, so analytics never slow the redirect.",
